:13 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Fergus annotates my Conversation with Ross Douthat]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.fergusmccullough.com/p/annotating-ross-douthats-conversation">https://www.fergusmccullough.com/p/annotating-ross-douthats-conversation</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/friday-assorted-links-507.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=friday-assorted-links-507">Friday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.fergusmccullough.com/p/annotating-ross-douthats-conversation</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.fergusmccullough.com/p/annotating-ross-douthats-conversation</guid><pubDate>Fri, 07 Feb 2025 16:09:13 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Is DOGE leading an AGI revolution in government?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1779496516056465408/jS1l512R_200x200.jpg"></p><p>Dean W. Ball (@deanwball) on X</p><p>I concur with @hamandcheese that the correct way to understand DOGE is not as a cost-cutting or staff-firing initiative, but instead as an effort to prepare the federal government for AGI. Trump describing it as a potential “Manhattan Project” is more interesting in this light.</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/deanwball/status/1887145845893505110">https://x.com/deanwball/status/1887145845893505110</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/thursday-assorted-links-488.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=thursday-assorted-links-488">Thursday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/deanwball/status/1887145845893505110</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/deanwball/status/1887145845893505110</guid><pubDate>Thu, 06 Feb 2025 17:48:16 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[And on the legislative front…?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1471636921176801282/4J1MH9WE_200x200.jpg"></p><p>Jake Sherman (@JakeSherman) on X</p><p>Failure. Consider this: Republicans split reconciliation into two pieces. The first bill — defense, border and energy — passes and gets signed into law. But Smith ends up being right and it gets too complicated to move a large-scale tax cut package. Then Republicans and</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/JakeSherman/status/1887135272376156341">https://x.com/JakeSherman/status/1887135272376156341</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/thursday-assorted-links-488.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=thursday-assorted-links-488">Thursday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/JakeSherman/status/1887135272376156341</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/JakeSherman/status/1887135272376156341</guid><pubDate>Thu, 06 Feb 2025 17:48:16 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Claims about what Deep Research is good for]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GjDyqRVX0AIrvq1.jpg:large"></p><p>Sayash Kapoor (@sayashk) on X</p><p>Can Deep Research automate the work of research assistants? I compared OpenAI and Google Deep Research for assistance with an upcoming project. In the process, I realized what Deep Research is great at, where it fails, and why commentators have such diverging views on it. 🧵</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/sayashk/status/1887275315824660584?s=46">https://x.com/sayashk/status/1887275315824660584?s=46</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/thursday-assorted-links-488.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=thursday-assorted-links-488">Thursday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/sayashk/status/1887275315824660584?s=46</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/sayashk/status/1887275315824660584?s=46</guid><pubDate>Thu, 06 Feb 2025 17:48:16 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[The geneti